x?$" :question/worked-solution "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ which has order $4.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30abb0c4-1bff-50f9-9fca-c00eba3cf9ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422285} {:answer/id #uuid "ebe184a0-615a-5d44-a917-af918964b500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebe184a0-615a-5d44-a917-af918964b500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422286} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ba99938-250e-5ae5-bf0d-5fb8ffd0e043" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422423} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18a407fa-f792-5416-9af8-1c23d1ca46fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422287} {:answer/id #uuid "d6eee552-87f0-587e-9972-7d671e2c86b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6eee552-87f0-587e-9972-7d671e2c86b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422288} :answer-field/id #uuid "d07ca297-62a7-5e03-8fea-27d8baa6b5a5" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422424} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed3c95ff-45e3-5a8f-97c1-8b53553ad9b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422289} {:answer/id #uuid "5ae2557d-e9db-57cc-b828-db6340b0d291" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed3c95ff-45e3-5a8f-97c1-8b53553ad9b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422289} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fb037b0-ed04-5067-99cf-fa5cc12a74f1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422425}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1054102-ae5b-5062-8507-4d26889215d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-96672" :question/problem "Which of the following are second-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{″}{x}^{3}-2y{y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$\n- **II.** $3{y}^{″}{y}^{′}-2y=2{y}^{′}$\n- **III.** $4{y}^{‴}x-7{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y{e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is of second-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\n- Equation II is also a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\n- Equation III is not a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166652 :question/id #uuid "a2564a21-1cf4-572e-a33b-b8db4aa60dfa" :question/math-academy-id "e-8460" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=y^3$\n\n- $x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}- y^3=2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y}=1$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, $y'=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)= y^3.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{y}^{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{3}+2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)= \\dfrac{y^3+2}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)= 1+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167441 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06525551-8771-58aa-abe0-a9f198bc1187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422261} {:answer/id #uuid "3cbf9b44-e10e-530c-98cd-737312ed32b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cbf9b44-e10e-530c-98cd-737312ed32b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422262} :answer-field/id #uuid "13241252-12fa-5fc2-a7aa-1c53e17cb337" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422411} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a7ee4e9-11d0-5f10-a3df-a90cf4050692" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422263} {:answer/id #uuid "64afbc79-5ad4-597f-bed9-2806cd57a48b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64afbc79-5ad4-597f-bed9-2806cd57a48b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422264} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d0f2bfd-a2c5-5edc-b594-edd014ea404a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422412} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f33fba92-6be7-5fec-96aa-554a753fbfab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422265} {:answer/id #uuid "89e83999-951c-5d6a-b8b5-25333fd7dc61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f33fba92-6be7-5fec-96aa-554a753fbfab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422265} :answer-field/id #uuid "3da8c301-99da-5e5e-8435-46dc10bd41c5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422413}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e9b8880-609b-5a27-b50e-083098d2cbea" :question/math-academy-id "q-95984" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{y}$\n- **II.** $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0$\n- **III.** $2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy=\\sqrt{x}$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)={e}^{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3y.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ac40970-e6e7-5ee8-ac6c-1fac307ec4dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422273} {:answer/id #uuid "69bc2ad9-2521-5c14-b8db-bc8a36f5b9fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69bc2ad9-2521-5c14-b8db-bc8a36f5b9fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422274} :answer-field/id #uuid "c227025b-4dfb-5ce9-821e-510b71e1cdfc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422417} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e18e0e7-caac-5bdf-ac4b-225b115fa69d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422275} {:answer/id #uuid "0b188ba2-6c75-5d2c-925d-6863beb94c0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e18e0e7-caac-5bdf-ac4b-225b115fa69d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422275} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dba96b9-06a1-5a54-b796-7d298384987b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422418} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3384518d-7a79-55c7-9801-cb13d141faa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422277} {:answer/id #uuid "ce709378-3669-57f3-9116-a908fe234d65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce709378-3669-57f3-9116-a908fe234d65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422278} :answer-field/id #uuid "94d10848-dfc0-5e56-9cf1-aa09031f2696" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422419}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ca3e21f-6e14-592d-bc53-233d6a2ad52a" :question/math-academy-id "q-96387" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}={e}^{x}$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{y}=0$\n- **III.** $\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}-xy={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{y} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{3}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}-xy & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′} & =xy+{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2(xy+{e}^{x}) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=2(xy+{e}^{x}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aad13619-d7b5-5d2f-b25b-0ed6aa30151f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422231} {:answer/id #uuid "caaf15ce-b06c-57ed-abf8-2ab34f65a039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "caaf15ce-b06c-57ed-abf8-2ab34f65a039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422232} :answer-field/id #uuid "28a8174b-4d8d-5b7d-a5ff-4c39764dc726" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422396} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "760c1112-d0a9-5b72-84d1-6232c1e23202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422233} {:answer/id #uuid "af5af3e2-59d5-55dd-9167-d6287704596f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af5af3e2-59d5-55dd-9167-d6287704596f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422234} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1040e7e-ad47-5805-b4ad-f5639fd421cb" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422397} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12d177fa-8dbe-5db2-8b85-40d491d016f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422235} {:answer/id #uuid "115c9ae8-f49c-5c69-9889-f210b446b628" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12d177fa-8dbe-5db2-8b85-40d491d016f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422235} :answer-field/id #uuid "073faff4-fa7e-5885-b9cf-d9a41f7de5bb" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422398}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98fb69b1-0fe2-5868-8c3d-537e50a23e41" :question/math-academy-id "q-118863" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{'}-8{e}^{y}=2$\n- **II.** $y{y}^{'}+3=\\operatorname{cos}y$\n- **III.** $x{y}^{'}-8={e}^{y}$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}-8{e}^{y} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{'} & =8{e}^{y}+2 \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=8{e}^{y}+2.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{'}+3 & =\\operatorname{cos}y \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}y-3}{y} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}y-3}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{'}-8 & ={e}^{y} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\frac{{e}^{y}+8}{x} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{y}+8}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234492 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24ca1a41-55a5-59ad-9001-0e2459c8bb6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422297} {:answer/id #uuid "e4011267-674e-5cd5-a96a-edbdb7f51458" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422298}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24ca1a41-55a5-59ad-9001-0e2459c8bb6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422297} :answer-field/id #uuid "33de995d-3b57-550c-9c7d-aa966ecdaf48" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422429} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b73cc998-84c0-5fc9-b67a-7c22e493f437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422299} {:answer/id #uuid "a43ee464-225b-5bac-b829-4893ccf7ec82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a43ee464-225b-5bac-b829-4893ccf7ec82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422300} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1f86d8f-f572-5fe3-9a19-500e72f3ef0c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422430} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf0c55d7-5ac5-57f8-97e1-27c64bfea087" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422301} {:answer/id #uuid "03394c62-f0fb-510f-ad01-72010e84b8a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf0c55d7-5ac5-57f8-97e1-27c64bfea087" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422301} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6038821-1293-59ab-9ec7-324905ffa46c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422431}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cb765ab-56a4-5072-853c-55e0a9cdf71b" :question/math-academy-id "q-96702" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x={e}^{y}$\n- **II.** $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2=\\sqrt{y}$\n- **III.** $4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{x}{y}=5y$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x & ={e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{y}+x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={e}^{y}+x.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2 & =\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{y}+2}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{y}+2}{2}.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{x}{y} & =5y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{4}(5y+\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{4}(5y+\\frac{x}{y}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234493 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "232eaa66-0237-5dd3-a69f-3e3272efb858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422303} {:answer/id #uuid "11d01465-58b4-5e71-ad95-2ed8f89b7195" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "232eaa66-0237-5dd3-a69f-3e3272efb858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422303} :answer-field/id #uuid "da09c621-2ab3-5008-9fd4-8147a7d93986" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422432} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91da46cd-4fec-5066-ad81-e05c51f102c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422305} {:answer/id #uuid "73818976-acff-57a9-95f9-347242cb9a86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73818976-acff-57a9-95f9-347242cb9a86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422306} :answer-field/id #uuid "dceb4f87-957a-593d-8d26-547fd90cf20a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422433} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d54ec6f4-81ae-505d-a857-5706b473527e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422307} {:answer/id #uuid "f81d0c2e-9ec1-5b79-942c-6f32380ff6f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f81d0c2e-9ec1-5b79-942c-6f32380ff6f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422308} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b0c1e47-f563-527f-b385-60bdc727eba0" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422434}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ca7da4a-7115-52ea-963d-df4449aa6da2" :question/math-academy-id "q-96703" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3}=2$\n- **II.** $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-7{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{y}$\n- **III.** $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{ln}y$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2+3{y}^{3}}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{2+3{y}^{3}}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-7{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =7{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=7{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{ln}y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234494 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0488ffaa-acc4-55b1-84c6-7107a1c151cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422309} {:answer/id #uuid "c67a1cca-c082-592e-8ecb-e3117b7bb71d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c67a1cca-c082-592e-8ecb-e3117b7bb71d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422310} :answer-field/id #uuid "064f00e8-e3b6-52e0-8529-c9f0f14fd0ce" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422435} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50501def-4ff1-5a98-9e69-d4d1e9355ad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422311} {:answer/id #uuid "b69d5df5-08af-5c32-991c-9c15c44dd068" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50501def-4ff1-5a98-9e69-d4d1e9355ad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422311} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cdca183-2209-5d53-aefc-42d46c84ca21" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422436} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb6948d0-acc3-5286-8891-af6b60d2e31d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422313} {:answer/id #uuid "93a8855e-cb56-5720-b143-7887ce875bff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb6948d0-acc3-5286-8891-af6b60d2e31d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422313} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fedbad3-4114-58f9-b8c9-fe8ec7b61ebd" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422437}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "483de092-6fe5-5b9f-85ea-10c713e1d18a" :question/math-academy-id "q-96705" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3}=0$\n- **II.** $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$\n- **III.** $5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x}=0$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y}.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+2x.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234495 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "486cbf20-6ed1-5ea6-919b-5e3452e6e684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422321} {:answer/id #uuid "aafcaf30-4b85-5f57-a825-f8813f70bba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "486cbf20-6ed1-5ea6-919b-5e3452e6e684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422321} :answer-field/id #uuid "466ce07a-3407-5b72-8d1c-b0b7154f1088" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422441} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5bfc78a-3e28-567c-8ff8-90cd62f225d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422323} {:answer/id #uuid "c6099c92-190c-5449-992e-da3da0f24f7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5bfc78a-3e28-567c-8ff8-90cd62f225d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422323} :answer-field/id #uuid "aba0cf42-3e95-5a7b-8ea1-38a1d871a8d9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422442} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e6bf198-bac2-537d-ac1b-b42144e1064c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422325} {:answer/id #uuid "084202a6-2162-54d6-9598-13a0685564ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "084202a6-2162-54d6-9598-13a0685564ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422326} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bad82c9-2c1b-5905-9265-70409b24568a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422443}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad324671-5207-59c5-b2d9-048cb88806c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-96712" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{'}=1-\\sqrt{y}$\n- **II.** ${y}^{'}-\\operatorname{tan}y={e}^{2}$\n- **III.** $x{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{y}=4$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. It is of the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=1-\\sqrt{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}-\\operatorname{tan}y & ={e}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\operatorname{tan}y+{e}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\operatorname{tan}y+{e}^{2}.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{y} & =4 \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\frac{4y+1}{xy} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{4y+1}{xy}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "429ab1e6-1ea8-591f-8018-488e0328c633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422219} {:answer/id #uuid "8abbe2b3-d552-5095-beac-5364c9bb1f5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8abbe2b3-d552-5095-beac-5364c9bb1f5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422220} :answer-field/id #uuid "932c7315-fbec-5137-99ad-8b3d6abcc576" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422390} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae25728a-975e-5157-92a8-8d1a96f74031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422221} {:answer/id #uuid "8dabfa11-04b9-5367-929d-f3c2edf37075" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae25728a-975e-5157-92a8-8d1a96f74031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422221} :answer-field/id #uuid "29377ff2-9a08-5ae1-b11b-c0869a6af61d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422391} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d642f39-1ba7-582e-99be-e5783acf5bf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422223} {:answer/id #uuid "a488d4a0-572b-5e44-a907-0c207d0eb139" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a488d4a0-572b-5e44-a907-0c207d0eb139" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422224} :answer-field/id #uuid "674b78a9-e972-5af8-91cd-bcf759582826" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422392}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4fb73e0-2435-50c4-b136-c8ed7ccb224f" :question/math-academy-id "q-118858" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${e}^{2y}{y}^{′}+1=0$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}+\\sqrt{xy}=2$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2y}{y}^{′}+1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-{e}^{-2y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-{e}^{-2y}.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{xy} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2-\\sqrt{xy} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=2-\\sqrt{xy}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253520 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "205c2cd6-04ab-56ea-9090-bfc8e049d542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422225} {:answer/id #uuid "78ae71d2-619b-56f3-92bf-ecfeebf9c3b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "205c2cd6-04ab-56ea-9090-bfc8e049d542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422225} :answer-field/id #uuid "4adb79a2-2fca-5fcf-8dd7-6acc5f68f3a9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422393} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18e49df7-3c70-5cd8-beb4-ff959fd404aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422227} {:answer/id #uuid "38051905-c461-5911-8df5-70c9f959f1d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38051905-c461-5911-8df5-70c9f959f1d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422228} :answer-field/id #uuid "cacddabc-75dd-59ce-afe3-05d7d1ba8798" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422394} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a5168fb-5c6b-5a1a-8194-633d3b1f1dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422229} {:answer/id #uuid "f7168c22-e701-5df5-af8a-614a1a97b22e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a5168fb-5c6b-5a1a-8194-633d3b1f1dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422229} :answer-field/id #uuid "944c44c1-dc25-512a-858d-9642a5fec880" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422395}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81da2b48-aa38-5eb5-8b7d-567a07263315" :question/math-academy-id "q-118859" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}-3{x}^{2}=1$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}+6y={e}^{3y}$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-3{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+1.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+6y & ={e}^{3y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={e}^{3y}-6y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={e}^{3y}-6y.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253522 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2888360-9f66-5c23-981b-f079328c8636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422237} {:answer/id #uuid "d327d689-8c39-594c-96b9-58a570c5b701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2888360-9f66-5c23-981b-f079328c8636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422237} :answer-field/id #uuid "c873029d-589f-57a0-a4d0-8437caa76bc0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422399} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73b6cd63-9458-513b-b2aa-d028666ac3c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422239} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf41c95-b46b-5d57-a7e0-be741ba1e4f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73b6cd63-9458-513b-b2aa-d028666ac3c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422239} :answer-field/id #uuid "d843cc15-36f5-5f93-b122-c8351e2ae5d6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422400} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa6421fe-c55f-5658-bf62-c0dde5e44e3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422241} {:answer/id #uuid "9a24fdb8-a81b-5920-a551-f1e49499aba2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a24fdb8-a81b-5920-a551-f1e49499aba2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422242} :answer-field/id #uuid "d809c0ac-7d83-52cd-b318-f7087cf971e1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422401}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40dc1226-9a4e-5dff-a14a-c81909c8002d" :question/math-academy-id "q-118868" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${x}^{2}{y}^{′}+6x{y}^{2}=8$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x}=3{y}^{2}$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({y}^{2}+2)$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+6x{y}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{8-6x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{8-6x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x} & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =3{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=3{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({y}^{2}+2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253523 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd904cf8-8e31-5dfb-a522-bc91b00cd7d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422243} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6f67a5-b49f-5fbd-8497-f7c6a4031fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd904cf8-8e31-5dfb-a522-bc91b00cd7d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422243} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd43700f-31c2-5c61-b73c-b13e37eff052" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422402} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d289e9c-3443-581d-a43b-b8699cb35799" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422245} {:answer/id #uuid "27ec5b50-7d97-5078-8643-a6c4784da9bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27ec5b50-7d97-5078-8643-a6c4784da9bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422246} :answer-field/id #uuid "12514015-bd25-5ca0-9c7a-27d17e773778" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422403} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6565a714-41fd-50da-aa58-7c4bb033f40d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422247} {:answer/id #uuid "c6441d94-73d1-5d09-a421-cb127ecdc684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6441d94-73d1-5d09-a421-cb127ecdc684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422248} :answer-field/id #uuid "3304dbae-d540-59d7-9271-398328295560" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422404}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f1c949f8-b9ab-5207-8749-bf7052ce14f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-118869" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi }=0$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}+{y}^{2}={x}^{2}$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi } & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi } \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi }.$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253524 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd1ea97b-c130-5985-8c17-cc72347ebe97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422249} {:answer/id #uuid "1f2223d5-e7e4-52cb-b31c-fde19140dc3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd1ea97b-c130-5985-8c17-cc72347ebe97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422249} :answer-field/id #uuid "abe17e70-19b7-564c-8b0d-d7279deacc98" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422405} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b4a5bf0-ec42-5cdd-9f4a-eafa46fc3dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422251} {:answer/id #uuid "70a78532-3820-5a35-b1c4-80a7950ecbc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b4a5bf0-ec42-5cdd-9f4a-eafa46fc3dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422251} :answer-field/id #uuid "006b96f3-1130-5007-b040-c8341e58057b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422406} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4e37f4d-fa5b-5fe7-96e5-e9ef0b93d247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422253} {:answer/id #uuid "73afeb6e-bb5d-5e60-a750-aabc89815638" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73afeb6e-bb5d-5e60-a750-aabc89815638" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422254} :answer-field/id #uuid "c68d0e4a-42ad-5fc6-a993-d34e08e87259" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422407}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e075483-5dc5-5bbb-be2d-e2d1164eea57" :question/math-academy-id "q-118870" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}={e}^{3-y}-2$\n- **II.** $y{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=1$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+y)$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. It is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)={e}^{3-y}-2.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c8c0cda-78bf-55f7-88e5-b25b198bc750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422315} {:answer/id #uuid "02410bef-7524-5134-a4f8-ebab62b11469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02410bef-7524-5134-a4f8-ebab62b11469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422316} :answer-field/id #uuid "264fc7d0-805e-5bdb-83e7-23bd58448ad6" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422438} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "447cd238-ce2a-5aa7-aa7b-1cd804b8d54f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422317} {:answer/id #uuid "a248c664-9709-57ad-a3ee-d65103a93863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a248c664-9709-57ad-a3ee-d65103a93863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422318} :answer-field/id #uuid "661ef71b-f6a7-5216-a51b-7056fd0d53e9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422439} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abdd71bd-d2a4-550e-bfe3-41eb169f5bfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422319} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef1a9b6-6b2d-597b-9ef5-e76a8a9c1843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abdd71bd-d2a4-550e-bfe3-41eb169f5bfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422319} :answer-field/id #uuid "4aa5233b-2a0d-5987-9db1-ae437c736ff9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422440}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5388ae42-2792-5587-8853-f61a559c11a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-96711" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}=1$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}y+{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{e}^{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}+1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b017fb70-0c5d-548a-9f29-f8c54647f96a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422327} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c9333b-f353-5073-a8ae-7603ecd03ae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b017fb70-0c5d-548a-9f29-f8c54647f96a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422327} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f6f0560-1c9e-5df2-9134-4ff3ff9b0077" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422444} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05aaa1e5-c77a-5c69-9336-ba40efd3dca5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422329} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc4734c-e2bd-5c34-bcd3-d7f463c81508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebc4734c-e2bd-5c34-bcd3-d7f463c81508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422330} :answer-field/id #uuid "a638c313-64c2-5397-bf14-121c910b5d46" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422445} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82717268-aeb7-59c7-9ead-ed0bccb337be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422331} {:answer/id #uuid "7d4573cc-8220-585b-b628-542222050b7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82717268-aeb7-59c7-9ead-ed0bccb337be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422331} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ec4389f-6741-54f0-af32-ea647439c22b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422446}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83ac0667-a1bc-5ca7-903e-f359054c0435" :question/math-academy-id "q-96713" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${e}^{-y}{y}^{′}=-\\sqrt{y}$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}-{y}^{3}=2{e}^{x}$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}=3\\sqrt{y}$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y}{y}^{′} & =-\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\sqrt{y}{e}^{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-\\sqrt{y}{e}^{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{y}^{3} & =2{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={y}^{3}+2{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={y}^{3}+2{e}^{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}} & =3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} 