-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422449}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb1a3b53-c6cd-51f3-bcd2-63da1f7e9756" :question/math-academy-id "q-96715" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\sqrt{2y}$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}-{y}^{3}=3\\sqrt{y}$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. It is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\sqrt{2y}.$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{y}^{3} & =3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={y}^{3}+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={y}^{3}+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}]}
